WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.800
Good Morning John.

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.620
I've got a problem.

00:00:01.620 --> 00:00:05.860
I will still be alive in 2060, probably.

00:00:05.860 --> 00:00:09.520
That's when the population will probably peak at around 10 billion people and also

00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:11.220
it is probably when we are gonna start feeling

00:00:11.220 --> 00:00:13.580
the really significant effects of global climate change.

00:00:13.580 --> 00:00:15.580
That's not a problem that is gonna be solved in the next 50 years.

00:00:15.580 --> 00:00:17.860
It's gonna be a problem when I die. And when you die.

00:00:17.860 --> 00:00:20.740
And lots of other problems will remain unsolved, and that's okay.

00:00:20.740 --> 00:00:24.340
Like, no one has ever died having solved all the problems.

00:00:24.340 --> 00:00:26.760
It was okay for them, it'll be okay for us.

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:30.680
But that doesn't mean that there isn't a path that we should be walking down to solve the problems eventually.

00:00:30.680 --> 00:00:34.620
But I feel like before we can start walking on that path, we have to sort of agree that there is a problem,

00:00:34.620 --> 00:00:37.020
which we apparently haven't done yet.

00:00:37.020 --> 00:00:39.960
So here are the top 10 arguments people use to say global warming either isn't a thing

00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:43.080
or isn't caused by humans and my responses to them:

00:00:43.080 --> 00:00:48.120
"Climate change is natural it's happened before". Yes, the climate has changed in the past.

00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:51.520
Often due to an increase in the carbon dioxide concentration in the atmosphere,

00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:55.840
which is actually a pretty clear indication that increase in carbon dioxide will increase temperatures.

00:00:55.840 --> 00:01:00.440
"But, Hank, CO2 concentration is only increased because temperature is increased, not the other way around".

00:01:00.440 --> 00:01:04.300
It is true that many warming events began without carbon dioxide levels increasing first;

00:01:04.300 --> 00:01:09.540
however, once the warming began, carbon dioxide levels increased, greatly amplifying the warming.

00:01:09.540 --> 00:01:15.440
Of the warming events we have record for, 90% of the warming took place after the carbon dioxide levels increased.

00:01:15.460 --> 00:01:18.080
"It's just that the sun's been getting hotter". No!

00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:22.080
"The data these scientists are using are wrong because they put the thermometers in cities where

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:24.900
there is heat sources and asphalt to heat everything up."

00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:29.320
The heating of the planet shows up when you look at weather stations in cities or in rural areas,

00:01:29.320 --> 00:01:33.360
it shows up when you look at satellite data or thermometers that are sunk deep in the ocean.

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:36.060
It's everywhere. Scientists are not stupid.

00:01:36.060 --> 00:01:41.420
"The earth is actually cooling." No, I mean it's not...it's not, at all.

00:01:41.420 --> 00:01:44.680
2000 to 2010 was the hottest decade ever. Ever on record.

00:01:44.680 --> 00:01:46.880
"But it's not warming up as fast".

00:01:46.880 --> 00:01:50.780
If you pick the years from 2009 'till now the trend line is indeed not as steep.

00:01:50.780 --> 00:01:53.120
But the climate doesn't change on the scale of years.

00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:57.660
That's like looking at a picture made of five pixels and trying to figure out what it is.

00:01:57.660 --> 00:02:02.960
It's just not going to be very clear. That's why we look at decades and centuries of data.

00:02:02.960 --> 00:02:05.380
"They predicted an ice age in the 70's".

00:02:05.380 --> 00:02:09.180
A small research team did say that and the news media talked about it for about two weeks.

00:02:09.180 --> 00:02:13.620
But the vast majority of peer-reviewed science during the 70's said that the climate was warming.

00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:17.740
Because of - you guessed it - carbon dioxide emissions.

00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:21.380
"You're just cherry-picking the data that support your hypothesis".

00:02:21.380 --> 00:02:24.140
Data supporting global warming comes from like every branch of science:

00:02:24.140 --> 00:02:29.760
whether it's biology, with animals ranging farther north, or epidemiology, with disease ranges spreading,

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:33.680
or agriculture, with yields increasing in temperate areas, increased water temperatures,

00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:37.420
decreased snowfall, earlier snowmelt, rising sea levels.

00:02:37.420 --> 00:02:40.180
"But Antarctica has more ice now than it used to".

00:02:40.180 --> 00:02:43.680
No. But interestingly, it does have more sea ice than it used to.

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:47.260
There is land ice, the ice that exists on the land of Antarctica (which is a continent),

00:02:47.260 --> 00:02:50.800
and then there is sea ice, which (for the most part) forms in the winter and melts in the summer.

00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:55.920
The land ice, the glaciers, is what you have to worry about, and that is melting at an alarming rate.

00:02:55.960 --> 00:03:02.080
Now, why there is more sea ice in Antarctica now than there has been previously is an interesting question. One that has been studied.

00:03:02.080 --> 00:03:04.860
And here is a quick description - you can pause the video to read it.

00:03:04.860 --> 00:03:08.460
"Fossil fuels... they're great". On this point, I agree with you.

00:03:08.460 --> 00:03:12.100
I think fossil fuels are miraculous. They are a precious resource.

00:03:12.100 --> 00:03:18.880
I'm not gonna rag on anyone who works hard providing inexpensive energy and the freedom and happiness that it provides.

00:03:18.880 --> 00:03:22.040
But there are costs to burning fossil fuels.

00:03:22.040 --> 00:03:25.520
So we have to use them less, by choice and through hard work.

00:03:25.520 --> 00:03:30.200
We have to develop alternatives, we have to be more efficient, and we have to change the way that we live our lives.

00:03:30.200 --> 00:03:35.640
But as long as we agree that there is a problem I have complete faith in humanity's ability to solve it.

00:03:35.640 --> 00:03:38.460
Because solving problems is what we do best.

00:03:38.620 --> 00:03:40.080
John, I'll see you on Tuesday.

